 \begin{abstract}
-Single-GPU mixture-of-experts (MoE) inference stores expert weights across memory tiers. Weight delivery introduces HDD reads and host-to-device transfers along the inference path. We present MECC, the MoE Expert Cache Compiler, to coordinate placement and replacement. The compiler allocates per-layer capacity and initializes expert residency from access profiles. The runtime promotes selected experts and retains GPU victims in host memory. Ordered reads and copies deliver weights for GPU execution. On one A100, MECC takes 7.38~s per decode token for FP16 Mixtral-8x7B with uniform initialization. Decode speedups are 1.77$\times$ over streaming and 2.95$\times$ over fixed CPU retention. Perplexity matches these controls.
+Expert offloading enables single-GPU inference for mixture-of-experts (MoE) models. Repeated HDD reads make expert residency a source of inference latency. We present MECC, the MoE Expert Cache Compiler, for profile-guided placement and joint HBM/CPU caching. Its compiler allocates slots by layer and emits the expert IDs used for initialization. The runtime promotes selected experts and retains displaced GPU weights in the host cache. Promotions and demotions update residency from the original router's selections. On one A100, MECC decodes FP16 Mixtral-8x7B at 7.38~s/token with uniform initialization. Decode speedups are 1.77$\times$ over streaming and 2.95$\times$ over fixed CPU retention, with matching perplexity.
 \end{abstract}
 
