+A cache entry identifies one expert's weights and its position in the resident order. HBM and host residency persist across layer calls. A later router selection can therefore reuse weights loaded by an earlier token. Calibration counts determine expert ranking; runtime accesses determine recency. Within one layer, sequences $[a,a,b,b]$ and $[b,b,a,a]$ have equal counts and different last-access orders. MECC uses both kinds of information.
 
 \section{Motivation and Insights}
 \label{sec:challenges}
 \subsection{Allocating Slots by Marginal Demand}
-A slot can cover different request mass in different layers. Uniform allocation gives each layer the same capacity, even after its hottest experts fit. Figure~\ref{fig:insights}(a,d) connects the resulting pressure to a per-layer capacity cut. The next uncovered expert determines the gain from another slot. Comparing that gain across layers allocates capacity where it covers more demand. Ranking within each layer then determines the initial IDs. Capacity and identity are separate outputs: one sets the room available to a layer, and the other fills it. Routing locality across models has also been studied independently~\cite{routingconsistency}.
+Equal expert counts do not imply equal demand across layers. A few ranks may cover most requests in one layer, while another layer spreads requests over more experts. Figure~\ref{fig:insights}(a,d) illustrates the resulting allocation choice. Its profile-guided plan shifts slots toward the layer groups with more cold demand. The compiler makes this choice by comparing each layer's next uncovered rank. Allocating a slot exposes that layer's following rank as a new candidate. This process distributes a shared allowance across layers and retains the IDs that fill each allocation. Routing locality across models has also been studied independently~\cite{routingconsistency}.
 
 \subsection{Preserving Reuse after GPU Eviction}
-A GPU eviction changes an expert's location before it determines the expert's next use. If the host retains the weights, another selection avoids HDD. Figure~\ref{fig:insights}(b,e) shows the promotion/demotion decision. Promotion vacates one CPU position as the HBM victim loses its GPU position. Joint retention reuses that host position for the victim. The independent-cache control reports 47.9\% HDD demand, versus 12.5\% for joint retention across all instrumented phases. The cache controls share an initial allocation and expert order. The victim keeps its place in the combined reuse order as it crosses the HBM boundary.
+GPU eviction is an admission decision for the host cache. Figure~\ref{fig:insights}(b,e) shows a CPU promotion vacating a host position as the GPU selects its victim. Joint retention admits that victim into the vacated position, carrying recency across the tier boundary. The independent-cache control reports 47.9\% HDD demand, compared with 12.5\% for the joint rule. Both start from the same allocation and expert order. The difference concerns how each policy retains the weights selected during execution. Preserving the victim lets a later selection use its host copy instead of initiating another cold read.
 
 \subsection{Updating Residency beyond Calibration}
-A profile summarizes calibration inputs. The next prompt can select a different expert set before a fixed initial placement changes. Figure~\ref{fig:insights}(c,f) pairs the demand shift with an update of resident IDs. The compiler supplies capacities and a starting membership. Recency then changes membership within those capacities. Initial coverage controls early reads; online admission controls the response to later selections. These are separate time intervals in one cache lifecycle. A task switch can expose the distinction even when both methods start with the same profile. Updating IDs from actual requests lets the runtime respond without compiling a new plan or predicting a later gate.
+Calibration and serving inputs can select different expert sets. In Figure~\ref{fig:insights}(c,f), a task switch changes demand while preserving the cache capacities. The fixed placement continues to retain its original expert set. Online admission instead brings newly selected experts into the resident order. Profile initialization lowers the early demand transient, and online replacement follows the subsequent switch. These effects occur at different points in the same request sequence. MECC therefore uses the profile to initialize membership and uses observed accesses to update it. The plan remains in place as resident IDs change.
 
